Hamnix Micro patch to Hamnix user/hamsh.ad (commit 1c5d976ac08fa89d58eb6a703db5f4153c5398a9).

Reason: the shell keeps its whole state in static arenas sized for a desktop:
4.16 MB of zero-initialised data, which on Hamnix Micro is the size of the block
every instance of the shell occupies (there is no demand paging: bss is real
memory from the first instruction).  This patch divides the four big arenas
and their dependent tables, and nothing else:

    string arena              1 MiB   -> 128 KiB   (STR_ARENA_MAX)
    AST nodes                 16384   -> 4096      (NODE_MAX, nd_* arrays)
    AST child-list slots      32768   -> 8192      (nd_kid*, written as a literal)
    value cells               16384   -> 4096      (VAL_MAX, val_* arrays)
    list/dict element slots   16384   -> 4096      (LISTELEM_MAX, val_elem)
    the collector's tables follow their arenas (gc_fwd_*, gc_stk_*, gc_owner)

The shell's own collector (gc_collect) compacts the arenas when they fill, and
its thresholds are written relative to these sizes, so they move with them.
Every changed line is a size literal or a message that quotes one; no logic
is changed.  Result: bss 4,163,664 -> 1,525,832 bytes.

Not changed, and why hamsh needs no patch for them:
  * fork: the kernel implements rfork(RFPROC) as vfork (kernel/proc.ad).
  * the GUI built-ins: lib/hamui.ad, lib/hamsdl*.ad are Hamnix Micro stand-ins.
  * the system calls: user/rt/rt.ad supplies Hamnix's names.

`make test` reverse-applies this patch to user/hamnix/hamsh.ad (minus its
3-line header) and requires the sha256 of the result to be the one recorded
for the original in fs/HAMNIX_ORIGIN.

